WEBVTT

q0001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.515
When a circuit has thousands of gates, what exact function is one output cone asking us to compute?

q0002
00:00:10.515 --> 00:00:21.030
This episode has one job: Circuit evaluation starts from gates and cones but must define semantic support, exact output, comparator structure, and task boundary before interpreting performance.

q0003
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:31.545
We build on CM, CSE, BitSet, BDD, SAT, Espresso, and SymPy: different questions, Live support versus ambient variables, Plain CSE versus sharing-aware CSE-flat; their definitions stay fixed while this lesson adds a new layer.

q0004
00:00:31.545 --> 00:00:42.060
A cone is the gate region that can influence one output; its semantic support is the subset of inputs that can change that output.

q0005
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:50.997
Watch for the label logic circuit; it stays attached to the artifact this episode means.

q0006
00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:58.813
When we say cone, the highlight identifies its layer before we interpret it.

q0007
00:00:58.813 --> 00:01:08.311
The term fanout receives its own visual state so it cannot drift into a neighboring concept.

q0008
00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:15.566
We will keep semantic support visible whenever its definition controls the inference.

q0009
00:01:15.566 --> 00:01:23.382
A persistent AND/INV label marks exactly where that object enters the example.

q0010
00:01:23.382 --> 00:01:33.897
The next sequence uses Small AND/INV cone as a conceptual teaching example, not as measured benchmark evidence.

q0011
00:01:33.897 --> 00:01:38.909
Our stable example is Small AND/INV cone.

q0012
00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:49.424
A conceptual five-input AND/INV cone whose output has four-variable semantic support, paired later with separately labeled retained EPFL evidence.

q0013
00:01:49.424 --> 00:01:56.679
Teach cone support, fanout, lowering, exact digests, and the accepted EPFL mechanism.

q0014
00:01:56.679 --> 00:02:05.616
We now construct the Small AND/INV cone and name every part before interpreting it.

q0015
00:02:05.616 --> 00:02:11.189
Now isolate circuit cone inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0016
00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:20.687
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for circuit cone changes.

q0017
00:02:20.687 --> 00:02:30.185
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to circuit cone, not to a substituted workload.

q0018
00:02:30.185 --> 00:02:38.001
A full circuit fades while one output cone and its fan-in remain bright.

q0019
00:02:38.001 --> 00:02:46.938
The cone starts from five named inputs, but the displayed output has four-variable semantic support.

q0020
00:02:46.938 --> 00:02:53.072
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate circuit cone.

q0021
00:02:53.072 --> 00:03:02.009
Structural counts describe nodes, edges, and operations; they do not directly measure hardware memory traffic.

q0022
00:03:02.009 --> 00:03:12.524
Any mechanism claim points to a visible change in the cone rather than to a benchmark ratio by itself.

q0023
00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:23.039
The starting state preserves the stable definition: A conceptual five-input AND/INV cone whose output has four-variable semantic support, paired later with separately labeled retained EPFL evidence.

q0024
00:03:23.039 --> 00:03:33.098
Zoom from a larger circuit into one output cone and trace which input variables can affect it.

q0025
00:03:33.098 --> 00:03:43.613
Lower the cone through CSE-flat and CM-IR, verify one truth digest, and connect its binary shape to the retained EPFL mechanism.

q0026
00:03:43.613 --> 00:03:49.747
Now isolate fanout and support inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0027
00:03:49.747 --> 00:03:59.806
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for fanout and support changes.

q0028
00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:09.865
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to fanout and support, not to a substituted workload.

q0029
00:04:09.865 --> 00:04:17.120
Semantic-support testing removes an ambient input that cannot influence the cone output.

q0030
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:23.814
Now isolate AND/INV workload shape inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0031
00:04:23.814 --> 00:04:34.329
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for AND/INV workload shape changes.

q0032
00:04:34.329 --> 00:04:44.844
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to AND/INV workload shape, not to a substituted workload.

q0033
00:04:44.844 --> 00:04:50.978
Matched gate graph, lowered instructions, and exact digest remain synchronized.

q0034
00:04:50.978 --> 00:04:57.112
Now isolate exact circuit controls inside Small AND/INV cone.

q0035
00:04:57.112 --> 00:05:07.171
The example definition and output meaning stay fixed while only the state for exact circuit controls changes.

q0036
00:05:07.171 --> 00:05:17.230
That matched before-and-after view assigns the visible consequence to exact circuit controls, not to a substituted workload.

q0037
00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:27.745
A circuit cone's semantic support and gate structure determine the exact function under study; nominal circuit size is not a substitute for cone support.

q0038
00:05:27.745 --> 00:05:35.561
Read that statement only within this scope: accepted EPFL AND/INV cone workload.

q0039
00:05:35.561 --> 00:05:40.012
Its declared measurement boundary is workload definition.

q0040
00:05:40.012 --> 00:05:45.585
The uncertainty field says corpus and support range scoped.

q0041
00:05:45.585 --> 00:05:56.100
On the accepted EPFL AND/INV workload, CM/CSE-flat was 0.9998 with a circuit-clustered 95% interval [0.9747, 1.0249].

q0042
00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:06.615
Read that statement only within this scope: EPFL AND/INV cones, semantic support 8–16, one Windows machine.

q0043
00:06:06.615 --> 00:06:12.749
Its declared measurement boundary is compiled evaluator kernel after compilation.

q0044
00:06:12.749 --> 00:06:17.761
The uncertainty field says circuit-clustered bootstrap, 4000 draws.

q0045
00:06:17.761 --> 00:06:28.276
On the EPFL AND/INV workload, CM and CSE-flat had equal instruction and executed-operation counts, matching the parity mechanism prediction.

q0046
00:06:28.276 --> 00:06:34.970
Read that statement only within this scope: EPFL AND/INV cones.

q0047
00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:41.104
Its declared measurement boundary is compiled program structure and kernel.

q0048
00:06:41.104 --> 00:06:47.238
The uncertainty field says exact count equality on accepted corpus.

q0049
00:06:47.238 --> 00:06:57.753
Corrected benchmark rows required frozen truth verification and equality across eligible timed arms before performance evidence was accepted.

q0050
00:06:57.753 --> 00:07:03.887
Read that statement only within this scope: corrected benchmark protocols.

q0051
00:07:03.887 --> 00:07:10.021
Its declared measurement boundary is correctness gate outside performance claim.

q0052
00:07:10.021 --> 00:07:14.472
The uncertainty field says hash/equality gate.

q0053
00:07:14.472 --> 00:07:24.987
The nearest confusing lesson is Configuration and feature-model workloads; it owns feature constraints, while this episode owns circuit cone.

q0054
00:07:24.987 --> 00:07:35.046
AND and inversion nodes form a small exact dependency cone whose fanout remains visible throughout the lesson.

q0055
00:07:35.046 --> 00:07:41.740
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate fanout and support.

q0056
00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:50.117
The inert input stays outlined so nominal width cannot be mistaken for active support.

q0057
00:07:50.117 --> 00:07:57.372
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate AND/INV workload shape.

q0058
00:07:57.372 --> 00:08:05.749
A topological trace orders the gates without changing their connectivity or the output function.

q0059
00:08:05.749 --> 00:08:12.443
Holding that element fixed lets this episode isolate exact circuit controls.

q0060
00:08:12.443 --> 00:08:21.380
An exact output digest binds the conceptual cone to any separately labeled retained measurement panel.

q0061
00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:31.439
The conceptual mechanism and EPFL evidence use matched colors but retain distinct status badges and source footers.

q0062
00:08:31.439 --> 00:08:40.937
Lowering or packing may change the execution artifact while the cone's admitted Boolean result remains fixed.

q0063
00:08:40.937 --> 00:08:49.874
The comparison closes with the same inputs, output contract, and exactness gate on every path.

q0064
00:08:49.874 --> 00:09:00.389
The accepted parity result belongs to selected EPFL AND/INV cones, not to all circuits or all tasks.

q0065
00:09:00.389 --> 00:09:10.904
Identify the gates and variables outside one selected cone and predict whether removing them changes its output function.

q0066
00:09:10.904 --> 00:09:20.963
Measure the function of the cone you actually selected, not the size of the circuit around it.

q0067
00:09:20.963 --> 00:09:31.478
A common mistake is this: Nominal circuit size directly determines the semantic support and strongest evaluation method for every cone.

q0068
00:09:31.478 --> 00:09:37.612
Repair it by returning to the owned distinction: circuit cone.

q0070
00:09:41.117 --> 00:09:51.632
Use the episode's central distinction to answer: Measure the function of the cone you actually selected, not the size of the circuit around it.

q0071
00:09:51.632 --> 00:10:00.009
Circuit structure becomes evidence only after the cone, support, output, and comparator are fixed.
